  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Chef Victor wants to impress someone with his French friend Jacques. Jacques mumbles in French and tries to kiss Victor. Victor stops him and offers him some food first. Let us listen to their conversation in Russian first.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте мы попробуем это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Жак, ты где?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Я здесь, я иду!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А вы кто такой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он мой сосед.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Представляешь, мы жили в Париже.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, мы не будем целоваться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сначала надо закусить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "На, попробуй это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Это очень вкусно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Он говорит, что это вкусно?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, ему очень нравится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Спасибо, мой дорогой друг.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пожалуйста, кушай на здоровье.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте мы попробуем это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's try this.' The first content word means 'let's', and 'this' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Жак, ты где?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Jacques, where are you?' The question opens with the interrogative word for 'where' and builds toward 'where'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Я здесь, я иду!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am here, I am coming!' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'going'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А вы кто такой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And who are you?' The question opens with the interrogative word for 'who' and builds toward 'such'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он мой сосед.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He is my neighbor.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'he' with 'neighbour'. Then Dmitry responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Представляешь, мы жили в Париже.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Can you imagine, we lived in Paris.' Here the verb for 'to live' takes the plural past-tense ending '-li', which is also used for the polite 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, мы не будем целоваться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait, we are not going to kiss.' Notice the reflexive ending on the verb for 'to kiss', which directs the action back onto the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сначала надо закусить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'First we need to have a bite.' Notice how 'first' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'have a bite'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "На, попробуй это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here, try this.' Chef Viktor combines 'for' with 'this' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Это очень вкусно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is very delicious.' A compact expression built around 'this' and 'it's tasty'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Он говорит, что это вкусно?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He says that it is delicious?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'it's tasty'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, ему очень нравится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, he likes it very much.' The reflexive ending on the verb for 'is pleasing' turns the action back onto the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Спасибо, мой дорогой друг.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you, my dear friend.' The character combines 'thank you' with 'friend' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пожалуйста, кушай на здоровье.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are welcome, eat to your health.' Chef Viktor combines 'please' with 'health' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
